From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NickelOS: the Bluetooth name is the computer's name

Other devices saw every NickelOS computer as NickelOS_XXXX over Bluetooth
(0056; Chromebook_XXXX before it). Once the computer has a name (Settings
> About, 0083), the adapter takes it: the hostname, as gethostname() gives
it without a file read on the UI thread. Until then (the hostname is
still localhost) it stays NickelOS_XXXX.

Chrome names the adapter each time Bluetooth turns on; Settings sets it
again right after a rename, so the new name shows without that.

diff --git a/ash/constants/devicetype.cc b/ash/constants/devicetype.cc
index 089eca93ed6df..2aeb62e731cdf 100644
--- a/ash/constants/devicetype.cc
+++ b/ash/constants/devicetype.cc
@@ -4,6 +4,11 @@
 
 #include "ash/constants/devicetype.h"
 
+#include <limits.h>
+#include <unistd.h>
+
+#include <string_view>
+
 #include "ash/constants/ash_switches.h"
 #include "base/command_line.h"
 #include "base/hash/hash.h"
@@ -21,6 +26,14 @@ constexpr char kDefaultDeviceTypeName[] = "NickelOS";
 }  // namespace
 
 std::string GetDeviceBluetoothName(const std::string& bluetooth_address) {
+  // NickelOS: the computer's name (Settings > About, nickel-hostnamed) once
+  // one is set; while the hostname is still localhost, NickelOS_XXXX.
+  char host[HOST_NAME_MAX + 1] = {};
+  if (gethostname(host, HOST_NAME_MAX) == 0 && host[0] &&
+      std::string_view(host) != "localhost") {
+    return host;
+  }
+
   const std::string device_name = DeviceTypeToString(chromeos::GetDeviceType());
 
